**Read:** [Quantum Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Quantum-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_0GY6BHCRA9CR5YGBY1AT9DRZY0), the remainder of chapters 2–3 and chapter 4 through the hydrogen atom. Work bound-state, scattering and angular-momentum calculations before the later optical and magnetic studies.

A finite barrier permits a decaying wave inside a classically forbidden region. Transmission is a flux ratio, so unequal incident and outgoing velocities matter. Bound states, propagating states and resonances answer different boundary-value questions. An effective mass and potential used for a semiconductor interface are model quantities, not a claim that the lattice has disappeared.

Atomic orbital and spin labels describe allowed states and symmetries; occupation and interactions determine which transitions or moments appear. Confinement can shift levels, but an observed optical shift can also reflect strain, chemistry or defects.

**Work:** Match a finite-well or barrier solution and check current balance. Compare an exact result with a thick-barrier approximation. Draw a proposed confinement experiment and identify a competing material-state explanation for the same signal. Explain where a one-electron atomic picture needs correction.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
